WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.825
Federal guidance asks
for more than completion.

2
00:00:02.825 --> 00:00:07.341
A-130 asks agencies to test users'
knowledge, and NIST's learning-program

3
00:00:07.341 --> 00:00:11.550
guide recommends practical
assessments that mimic the work role.

4
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.582
So the program certifies
only what a person can show,

5
00:00:14.582 --> 00:00:17.050
with an artifact someone else could check.

6
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.400
Every capstone is scored
on one four-level scale.

7
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.675
Zero is missing.

8
00:00:21.675 --> 00:00:25.800
One names the right books but gets
the controls or the evidence wrong.

9
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.500
Two traces the chain from book
to control to evidence source.

10
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.713
Three is assessor-ready:
a real, dated result,

11
00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:36.550
with an honest statement of
what isn't evaluated yet.

12
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.679
The compliance capstone passes
with every area at two or better,

13
00:00:40.679 --> 00:00:44.575
at least three at three, and all
four whole-submission checks.

14
00:00:44.575 --> 00:00:47.742
An after-action report with
no findings scores zero,

15
00:00:47.742 --> 00:00:52.013
and so does a training percentage
with no per-person records behind it.

16
00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:57.088
Two credentials follow: practitioner for
builders, assessor for compliance staff.

17
00:00:57.088 --> 00:00:59.212
Both expire after twelve months.

18
00:00:59.212 --> 00:01:03.360
An industry certification can waive
a module, but never a capstone.
